From: =?UTF-8?q?J=C3=A1nos=20Mik=C3=B3?=
 <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 14:08:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 075/154] refactor(core): split mutate.rs by subsystem, and
 raise the grd id range
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mutate.rs ran 3336 lines and held fourteen unrelated concerns, so nothing
local told a reader that sweep_archive and set_sidebar_width belonged to
different subsystems. Its module doc described only index translation, which
by then covered a fifth of the file.

Eight submodules, each with a doc about what it actually holds: favorites
(with doorways, which share the per-profile rule), notes (with marginalia,
because a marginal IS a note about a page and shares its caps), shelf,
archive (archived and snoozed together - one operation, two reasons), groups,
space_settings, live_tabs, chrome. mod.rs keeps what the index rule applies
to: Section, the translation functions, the row moves and pins that use them,
space create/rename/reorder/active, locate_token. 1570 production lines
become 460 there.

Re-exported rather than re-pathed, so mutate::add_favorite still resolves.
Moving the code is a smaller change to review than moving the code and sixty
callers.

Tests moved with their code, attributed by which functions they CALL rather
than by name - a test named for the behaviour it checks does not mention the
function it drives. One spans subsystems and stays in mod.rs, where all the
caps it asserts are in scope. Their shared fixtures are one test_support
module, because a fixture that drifts makes two test modules agree about a
document the code never sees.

restore::apply's loop body becomes apply_slice: 134 lines nested four deep
become 31 and 121, with the five phases named. Its contract is still
all-or-nothing and still checked by eye, but over a function whose phases have
names. The two favourite accumulators become one value, because they are only
correct together - a profile listed as written with no rows CLEARS that
profile's grid.

The grd allocation goes from 70 to 90 because clock.js took the count to 71.
Grit fails at RESOURCE GENERATION, so the node suites cannot see it, which is
how that landed one over the line.

The BUILD.gn drift guard now recurses one level. It read only the top of src/,
so every file under a directory module would have escaped it - proven by
dropping notes.rs from the list and watching it fail.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 tools/gritsettings/resource_ids.spec | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tools/gritsettings/resource_ids.spec b/tools/gritsettings/resource_ids.spec
index ff404801ac217..f6096c56c9fe2 100644
--- a/tools/gritsettings/resource_ids.spec
+++ b/tools/gritsettings/resource_ids.spec
@@ -1810,7 +1810,11 @@
   # Atrium chrome layer. Placed at the end of this section so the
   # real IDs land after the placeholder block and nothing downstream realigns.
   "chrome/browser/resources/atrium/atrium_resources.grd": {
-    "META": {"sizes": {"includes": [70]}},
+    # Headroom, not a fit: the grd holds 71 includes and grit fails the BUILD
+    # when the count passes the allocation. It fails at RESOURCE GENERATION, so
+    # a change that only runs the node suites will not see it - which is how
+    # clock.js landed one over the line.
+    "META": {"sizes": {"includes": [90]}},
     "includes": [12020],
   },
 
